#limitation(title: [The Anticoagulation Inference Is Unsupported by Trial Evidence])[
The step from microclot biology to anticoagulation has been tested once at scale, and it did not show benefit.

The STIMULATE-ICP trial randomised 778 patients with the post-COVID-19 condition, none hospitalized with COVID-19, to rivaroxaban 10~mg once daily for 12 weeks, colchicine, famotidine plus loratadine, or specialist supportive clinical care alone @StimulateICP2026rivaroxaban. Rivaroxaban did not improve fatigue: the adjusted difference was $-1.06$ points (95% confidence interval $-2.47$ to $0.35$, $p = 0.139$). Colchicine ($-1.49$, $p = 0.041$) and famotidine-loratadine ($-1.48$, $p = 0.038$) reached nominal significance, but no group differed at 24 weeks, 12 weeks after the drugs stopped, and both are anti-inflammatory rather than anticoagulant interventions.

The remaining therapeutic route, plasmapheresis to remove the particles, has no randomised evidence at all: a Cochrane review found no study in which patients with the post-COVID-19 condition had undergone plasmapheresis @Cochrane2023Plasmapheresis.

Both pieces of evidence carry the same population restriction as the null coagulation study: never-hospitalized participants. That leaves severe and very-severe ME/CFS, the stratum where anticoagulation risk and microvascular compromise would matter most, with no trial evidence in either direction.

The limitation concerns the inference, not the mechanism. Microclot formation may be real and may still matter mechanically while anticoagulation remains the wrong intervention: a drug that reduces clot formation does not necessarily clear aggregates that are already amyloid-stabilised and fibrinolysis-resistant, and it adds bleeding risk in exchange. Anticoagulation for ME/CFS outside a trial is therefore unsupported, and the burden of proof sits with any protocol that proposes it. The defensible position is that the mechanism is unresolved and the intervention is untested in ME/CFS specifically; the Long COVID trial result is the closest available evidence and it is negative.

*Consequence:* Patients should not start anticoagulation for ME/CFS outside a clinical trial, and clinicians should not present microclot testing as a reason to start it.
